mls — provenance note
======================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bn254`/
`bls12_381`/`bip340`/`adaptor` modules — self-contained crypto/protocol
modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

1. **RFC 9420** (Messaging Layer Security,
   https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9420) — the source of truth for
   every algorithm in this module: §2.1/§2.1.2 (`codec.zig`'s
   presentation-language + QUIC-style variable-length-integer vector
   encoding), §5.1/§17.1 (`suite.zig`'s cipher-suite bundle and suite
   `0x0001`'s algorithm assignment), §5.1.2/§5.1.3/§5.2/§8/§9.1
   (`crypto.zig`'s `SignWithLabel`/`VerifyWithLabel`/`EncryptWithLabel`/
   `DecryptWithLabel`/`RefHash`/`ExpandWithLabel`/`DeriveSecret`/
   `DeriveTreeSecret`). A public IETF specification — not copyrightable
   expression (merger doctrine, `CONVENTIONS.md` §5) — so no attribution
   entry is strictly required for the spec citation alone; listed here
   for completeness alongside item 2's stronger case.

2. **RFC 9420 Appendix C** ("Array-Based Trees") specifically —
   `treemath.zig`'s `log2`/`level`/`node_width`/`root`/`left`/`right`/
   `parent`/`sibling`/`direct_path`/`copath` are a DIRECT PORT of the
   Python reference algorithm the RFC itself publishes in this appendix
   (not merely a description implemented independently — the RFC's own
   text frames this as runnable reference code, and this module
   translates it line-for-line into Zig, preserving the same variable
   names/structure where Zig syntax allows). Still public-specification
   text under the same merger-doctrine reasoning as item 1 (an RFC's
   own algorithm listing is part of the specification, not a separate
   third-party implementation), but called out explicitly here because
   the relationship is "port", not "clean-room from prose" — matching
   the standard this repo's `CONVENTIONS.md` §5 sets for when a design
   reference (even a public-spec one) is worth naming precisely.

